.TH "KCLEAN" 1 "2026-09-29" "toolbelt 0.1.0" "toolbelt manual"
.SH NAME
kclean \- delete evicted, completed and crashing pods
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
kclean [-n NAMESPACE | -A] [--only KIND] [--system] [-y] [-- kubectl delete options]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Pods pile up in a namespace. The kubelet evicts pods when a node runs short of memory or disk, and an evicted pod stays in the list with the status \fBEvicted\fR until someone deletes it. A CronJob leaves a finished pod behind for every run it keeps. A pod in CrashLoopBackOff keeps restarting and fills \fBkubectl get pods\fR with noise. None of them does anything useful, and all of them hide the pods you want to look at.
.PP
\fBkclean\fR finds those three kinds of pods, lists them grouped by kind, and asks once before it deletes them. The list names each pod, and for a crashing pod also its restart count and its owner. The owner tells you what happens after the delete. A pod owned by a Deployment, StatefulSet or DaemonSet comes back as a fresh copy, so deleting a crashing one is a restart. A pod with no owner is gone for good.
.PP
Pods cannot go to a trash. The question names the count and the namespace, and says so. Without a terminal to ask, \fBkclean\fR refuses unless you pass \fB\-y\fR. With \fB\-\- \-\-dry\-run=client\fR, kubectl reports what it would delete and nothing is deleted.
.PP
\fBkclean\fR never touches pods that are Running and healthy, Pending, or already being deleted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-namespace NS\fR
Clean this namespace. The context's own namespace by default.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-all\-namespaces\fR
Clean every namespace except kube\-system.
.TP
\fB\-\-only KIND\fR
Only \fBevicted\fR, \fBcompleted\fR or \fBcrashloop\fR pods. Repeat it for two kinds.
.TP
\fB\-\-system\fR
Include kube\-system. Needed with \fB\-A\fR to reach it, and with \fB\-n kube\-system\fR.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Delete without asking.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print only the result line.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
List every pod name, print each kubectl command before it runs, and show kubectl's own output.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help.
.PP
\fB\-A\fR and \fB\-n\fR together are a usage error. \fBkclean\fR takes no pod names. It finds the pods itself, and \fBkubectl delete pod NAME\fR is the tool for one pod you already know.
.SH WHAT COUNTS
.TP
\fBevicted\fR
What \fBkclean\fR looks for: Status \fBFailed\fR with reason \fBEvicted\fR.. What deleting it does: Removes the record. The owner made a new pod when this one was evicted.
.TP
\fBcompleted\fR
What \fBkclean\fR looks for: Status \fBSucceeded\fR, such as a finished Job run.. What deleting it does: Removes the pod and its logs. The Job object stays.
.TP
\fBcrashloop\fR
What \fBkclean\fR looks for: Any container or init container waiting with reason \fBCrashLoopBackOff\fR.. What deleting it does: Restarts it when an owner makes a new one. Stops it for good when it has no owner.
.PP
Pods with a deletion time are already on their way out and never count.
.SH THE LIST
.PP
Each kind gets one line with its label and count, followed by the pod names. Names wrap at 72 columns, and a kind with more than three pods shows the first two and \fBand N more\fR. \fB\-v\fR shows every name.
.PP
Crashing pods get one line each, since the owner matters there. The owner comes from the pod's ownerReferences. A ReplicaSet made by a Deployment shows as the Deployment, such as \fBdeploy/api\fR, found by taking the \fBpod\-template\-hash\fR off the ReplicaSet name.
.PP
With \fB\-A\fR, names get a \fBnamespace/\fR prefix and the question counts the namespaces.
.SH SAFETY
.IP \(bu 2
It always asks first, with the count and the namespace, and says the pods cannot be restored.
.IP \(bu 2
No terminal and no \fB\-y\fR means exit 4 and nothing deleted. A cron job or script must pass \fB\-y\fR on purpose.
.IP \(bu 2
kube\-system holds the cluster's own pods, such as CoreDNS and the API server on kubeadm clusters. \fB\-A\fR skips it, and \fB\-n kube\-system\fR refuses with exit 4, unless you pass \fB\-\-system\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Deletes use \fB\-\-ignore\-not\-found\fR, so a pod that went away between the list and the delete is not an error.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\- \-\-dry\-run=client\fR or \fB\-\- \-\-dry\-run=server\fR skips the question and deletes nothing.
.SH ON DIFFERENT CLUSTERS
.PP
kind and minikube : Evicted pods are common, since the node is your laptop and shares its memory with everything else. Clean them after a heavy test run.
.PP
kubeadm clusters : The API server, etcd, the scheduler and the controller manager run as static pods in kube\-system. The kubelet owns them, so deleting one only makes the kubelet start it again. That is one more reason \fBkclean\fR skips kube\-system.
.PP
Managed clusters such as EKS, GKE and AKS : The provider runs its own agents in kube\-system and sometimes in namespaces of its own. \fB\-A\fR still visits those other namespaces, so read the list before you answer.
.SH PASS\-THROUGH
.PP
Options after \fB\-\-\fR go to \fBkubectl delete pod\fR. The list call gets only \fB\-\-context\fR and \fB\-\-kubeconfig\fR from them.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean --only completed -- --grace-period 0
$ kclean -n shop -- --dry-run=client
$ kclean -A -- --context kind-kind
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-\-grace\-period 0\fR matters for crashing pods, which may otherwise wait the full 30 seconds to stop.
.SH NEEDS
.PP
\fBkubectl\fR and \fBjq\fR. The account behind your context needs \fBlist\fR and \fBdelete\fR on pods in the namespaces it cleans.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Clean the current namespace
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean
context kind-kind, namespace shop
evicted    2  cart-7d9f8c6b54-8kq1r cart-7d9f8c6b54-v2m7t
completed  4  report-28791440-x7k2p report-28791500-m4n8q and 2 more
crashloop  2  api-5b8c9d7f6-mk4tn, restarts 14, owned by deploy/api
              debug-shell, restarts 6, no owner, it will not come back
Delete 8 pods in shop? They cannot be restored. [y/N] y
deleted 8 pods
.fi
.RE
.PP
The api pod comes back as a fresh copy. The debug\-shell pod had no owner, so it is gone.
.SS Every namespace, evicted pods only
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean -A --only evicted
context kind-kind, all namespaces, kube-system skipped
evicted    3  shop/cart-7d9f8c6b54-8kq1r shop/cart-7d9f8c6b54-v2m7t
              monitoring/grafana-5f7c-abcde
Delete 3 pods in 2 namespaces? They cannot be restored. [y/N] n
Nothing changed.
$ echo $?
5
.fi
.RE
.SS Dry run first
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean -v -n shop -- --dry-run=client
+ kubectl get pods -n shop -o json
context kind-kind, namespace shop
evicted    2  cart-7d9f8c6b54-8kq1r cart-7d9f8c6b54-v2m7t
completed  4  report-28791440-x7k2p report-28791500-m4n8q migrate-9fz2c
              report-28791560-q9w3e
crashloop  2  api-5b8c9d7f6-mk4tn, restarts 14, owned by deploy/api
              debug-shell, restarts 6, no owner, it will not come back
+ kubectl delete pod -n shop --ignore-not-found report-28791440-x7k2p report-28791500-m4n8q migrate-9fz2c report-28791560-q9w3e cart-7d9f8c6b54-8kq1r cart-7d9f8c6b54-v2m7t api-5b8c9d7f6-mk4tn debug-shell --dry-run=client
pod "report-28791440-x7k2p" deleted (dry run)
\&...
dry run, 8 pods would be deleted
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fB\-v\fR lists every completed pod, where the plain list stopped at two.
.SS From a script or cron job
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean -n shop -q -y --only completed
deleted 4 pods
.fi
.RE
.PP
Without \fB\-y\fR and without a terminal, the list prints and nothing is deleted.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean < /dev/null
context kind-kind, namespace shop
\&...
kclean: not asking without a terminal, pass --yes to go ahead
$ echo $?
4
.fi
.RE
.SS Nothing to do
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean -n monitoring --only completed
context kind-kind, namespace monitoring
nothing to clean in monitoring
$ echo $?
0
.fi
.RE
.SS kube\-system
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean -n kube-system
kclean: kube-system holds the cluster's own pods. Add --system to clean it
$ echo $?
4
$ kclean -A --system -y --only evicted
context kind-kind, all namespaces
evicted    4  shop/cart-7d9f8c6b54-8kq1r shop/cart-7d9f8c6b54-v2m7t
              and 2 more
deleted 4 pods
.fi
.RE
.SS Restart crashing pods at once
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean -n shop --only crashloop -y -- --grace-period 0
context kind-kind, namespace shop
crashloop  2  api-5b8c9d7f6-mk4tn, restarts 14, owned by deploy/api
              debug-shell, restarts 6, no owner, it will not come back
deleted 2 pods
.fi
.RE
.PP
The Deployment makes a new api pod with a restart count of 0, which resets the back\-off. Use it after you fixed what made the pod crash, such as a missing Secret.
.SS A kind it does not know
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ kclean --only oops
kclean: --only takes evicted, completed or crashloop, not oops
Try 'kclean --help' for the options.
.fi
.RE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.PP
Evicted pods come back after a while : The node is still short of memory or disk, so the kubelet keeps evicting. \fBknodes\fR shows which node is under pressure, and \fBkres\fR shows how full its requests are.
.PP
Completed pods come back after every run : That is the CronJob keeping its history. Set \fBsuccessfulJobsHistoryLimit\fR and \fBfailedJobsHistoryLimit\fR on the CronJob, or \fBttlSecondsAfterFinished\fR on the Job, and the cluster cleans them itself.
.PP
A crashing pod comes straight back as CrashLoopBackOff : Deleting it restarts it, which does not fix the cause. \fBkwhy\fR shows the exit code and the last log lines of the crashed container.
.PP
\fBkclean: kubectl delete failed in NS: ...\fR : The account may list pods but not delete them. The pods in other namespaces are still deleted, and \fBkclean\fR exits 1.
.PP
\fBkclean: the cluster refused: pods is forbidden ...\fR : The account may not list pods in that namespace. Pick a namespace with \fB\-n\fR, or a context with more rights.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Nothing to clean, the pods were deleted, or a dry run listed them.
.TP
1
kubectl failed, to list or to delete.
.TP
2
Bad usage, such as \fB\-A\fR with \fB\-n\fR, a pod name, or an unknown \fB\-\-only\fR kind.
.TP
3
kubectl or jq is missing.
.TP
4
Refused. No terminal to ask and no \fB\-y\fR, or kube\-system without \fB\-\-system\fR.
.TP
5
You answered no.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBkwhy\fR, \fBknodes\fR, \fBkres\fR, \fBkevents\fR, \fBkubectl\-delete(1)\fR
